The Healthcare Access Management aplication prototype I made was tested using the Ethereum Sepolia test network and dedicated test wallets. My testing focused on verifying the smart contract's permission management functions, invalid transaction handling and application integration.

Test 1 - patient grants doctor access to record 1
the expected result was that the permission becomes valid, and the test passed

Test 2 - patient checks granted permissions
the patient has access to check the permissions, the test passed

Test 3 - patient revokes access to record 1 
the access is revoked, doctor loses access, and the test passed

Test 4 - patient checks revoked permissions
the patient has access to check the permissions, the test passed

Test 5 - patient tries grant duplicate access
the transaction was expected to be rejected, and the transaction was rejected returning an 'access already granted' prompt

Test 6 - doctor attempts to grant access to themselves
transaction was supposed to be rejected, as only patient can do that, and the test passed

The prototype was tested on the Ethereum Sepolia test network rather than Ethereum mainnet. So, while the testing demonstrates the expected behaviour of the implemented functions but does not constitute a full independent security audit.
